1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:06,763
Hi, I'm Kunal Kumar, roll number 24BCS10027.

2
00:00:06,713 --> 00:00:10,347
This is my DevOps capstone, ClinicFlow.

3
00:00:10,347 --> 00:00:13,785
It's an appointment desk for a small clinic.

4
00:00:13,785 --> 00:00:17,902
In this video I'll show the app, then make a real change, push

5
00:00:17,902 --> 00:00:21,953
it, and follow it all the way through the pipeline until it's

6
00:00:21,953 --> 00:00:23,414
running on Kubernetes.

7
00:00:24,470 --> 00:00:26,411
So this is the app.

8
00:00:26,361 --> 00:00:30,254
Reception books a patient in with a doctor, and marks the

9
00:00:30,254 --> 00:00:33,328
appointment completed, cancelled, or no show.

10
00:00:33,328 --> 00:00:37,309
The one rule that really matters is that a doctor can't be

11
00:00:37,309 --> 00:00:38,270
double booked.

12
00:00:38,270 --> 00:00:43,907
If two slots overlap, the API says no, with a 409.

13
00:00:43,907 --> 00:00:47,934
Down in the sidebar it shows the version, the commit, and

14
00:00:47,934 --> 00:00:49,277
where it's running.

15
00:00:49,277 --> 00:00:53,386
Right now that's version 0.1.0.

16
00:00:53,386 --> 00:00:55,493
Keep an eye on that.

17
00:00:56,550 --> 00:01:01,363
Behind it, there's a React frontend served by Nginx, a FastAPI

18
00:01:01,363 --> 00:01:05,944
backend, and Postgres, with the table created by an Alembic

19
00:01:05,944 --> 00:01:06,720
migration.

20
00:01:06,670 --> 00:01:10,466
Locally, the whole thing runs with docker compose.

21
00:01:10,466 --> 00:01:14,257
In the pipeline, the same images are deployed with a Helm

22
00:01:14,257 --> 00:01:18,381
chart onto a Kubernetes cluster, with an Ingress in front, and

23
00:01:18,381 --> 00:01:20,709
Prometheus and Grafana watching it.

24
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:24,002
Okay, the live change.

25
00:01:23,952 --> 00:01:28,468
I'm bumping the version to 0.2.0, in the Helm chart, the

26
00:01:28,468 --> 00:01:31,452
compose file, and the backend config.

27
00:01:31,452 --> 00:01:34,924
Commit, push, and that's it from my side.

28
00:01:34,924 --> 00:01:38,049
Nothing after this is done by hand.

29
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:42,140
The push starts the pipeline straight away.

30
00:01:42,090 --> 00:01:46,755
Tests and lint run first, along with terraform validate.

31
00:01:46,755 --> 00:01:50,354
Only if those pass does it build the images.

32
00:01:50,354 --> 00:01:54,334
Then it brings the full stack up with docker compose, and

33
00:01:54,334 --> 00:01:56,708
finally deploys to a kind cluster.

34
00:01:56,708 --> 00:02:01,477
The whole run takes about eleven minutes, so this part is sped

35
00:02:01,477 --> 00:02:01,708
up.

36
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:05,948
Here's what those jobs actually did.

37
00:02:05,898 --> 00:02:10,318
All fourteen tests passed, including the double booking ones,

38
00:02:10,318 --> 00:02:12,564
on a throwaway SQLite database.

39
00:02:12,564 --> 00:02:17,048
Then each image is scanned with Trivy, and the gate fails the

40
00:02:17,048 --> 00:02:21,384
build on any high or critical vulnerability that has a fix.

41
00:02:21,384 --> 00:02:25,395
Only after that are the images pushed to the GitHub container

42
00:02:25,395 --> 00:02:28,814
registry, tagged with the commit hash, never latest.

43
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:31,870
Then the deploy job.

44
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:36,342
Helm upgrades the release, and kubectl shows two backend pods,

45
00:02:36,342 --> 00:02:39,552
two frontend pods and Postgres, all running.

46
00:02:39,552 --> 00:02:43,817
And this is the app, opened through the Ingress, after the

47
00:02:43,817 --> 00:02:44,332
deploy.

48
00:02:44,332 --> 00:02:49,829
The sidebar now says version 0.2.0, and the commit is the one

49
00:02:49,829 --> 00:02:51,091
I just pushed.

50
00:02:51,091 --> 00:02:55,091
So the change went from my laptop to the cluster without me

51
00:02:55,091 --> 00:02:56,311
touching anything.

52
00:02:57,380 --> 00:03:02,101
For AWS, the terraform folder describes a VPC with public and

53
00:03:02,101 --> 00:03:06,822
private subnets across two availability zones, a NAT gateway,

54
00:03:06,822 --> 00:03:10,305
and an EKS cluster with a managed node group.

55
00:03:10,255 --> 00:03:14,110
The pipeline validates it and makes a plan, twenty two

56
00:03:14,110 --> 00:03:15,324
resources to add.

57
00:03:15,324 --> 00:03:19,879
To be upfront, I planned it but didn't apply it, because EKS

58
00:03:19,879 --> 00:03:21,701
costs money by the hour.

59
00:03:22,760 --> 00:03:24,435
And monitoring.

60
00:03:24,385 --> 00:03:28,997
The backend exposes a metrics endpoint, and Prometheus scrapes

61
00:03:28,997 --> 00:03:31,005
it, so the target shows up.

62
00:03:31,005 --> 00:03:36,037
Grafana has a dashboard for the API, with request rate by

63
00:03:36,037 --> 00:03:41,511
endpoint, p95 latency, the 5xx rate, and how many backend pods

64
00:03:41,511 --> 00:03:42,394
are ready.

65
00:03:43,460 --> 00:03:45,493
So that's ClinicFlow.

66
00:03:45,443 --> 00:03:49,998
Code on GitHub, tests and security scans on every push, images

67
00:03:49,998 --> 00:03:54,553
in the registry, infrastructure as code, and a Helm deploy you

68
00:03:54,553 --> 00:03:55,802
can watch update.

69
00:03:55,802 --> 00:03:57,712
Thanks for watching.
